<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1100 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Salve Regina</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Latin evening hymn to Mary from the Middle Ages, sung by monks on the way to bed and much loved by sailors.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous (traditionally ascribed to Hermann of Reichenau, Bernard of Clairvaux, Peter of Compostela, Adhemar of Le Puy and others)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (Gregorian chant)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Latin Christians of western Europe</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>not known; set down in largely its current form at the Abbey of Cluny, present-day France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>Middle Ages; current form set down at Cluny in the 12th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics anonymous (Middle Ages; traditionally ascribed to Hermann of Reichenau, 11th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salve-regina">Salve Regina</a> (&quot;Hail, Queen&quot;), also known as the Hail Holy Queen, is a Marian hymn and one of four Marian antiphons sung at different seasons of the Catholic Church's year. It was composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Tradition has ascribed it to the eleventh-century German monk Hermann of Reichenau, and also to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a>, Peter of Compostela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adhemar-of-le-puy">Adhemar of Le Puy</a>, among others, but most musicologists regard it as anonymous.</p><p>Marian antiphons have been sung at the close of Compline, the last Office of the day, since the thirteenth century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salve-regina">Salve Regina</a> is the best known of the four. Its place after Compline probably goes back to the monastic custom of intoning it in chapel and chanting it on the way to the sleeping quarters. It was set down in largely its present form at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluny-abbey">Abbey of Cluny</a> in the 12th century, where it served as a processional hymn on Marian feasts, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cistercians">Cistercians</a> chanted it daily from 1218. The text sung today is almost exactly the original: one word of the first line seems to have been added in the 16th century, and one of the last line in the 13th.</p><p>In the 1220s or 1230s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friars</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> adopted a nightly Salve procession at Compline, and the practice soon spread through the whole order; because the Dominicans were already spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the hymn's popularity is largely credited to them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a>, on the advice of the Dominican Raymond of Penyafort, introduced it in the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on Fridays at Compline, and many dioceses and orders took it up during the 13th century. It was also popular at medieval universities as an evening song, became part of the ritual for blessing a ship, and was especially dear to sailors.</p><p>Later centuries gave it new uses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vi">Pope Pius VI</a> attached an indulgence to its recitation by a decree of 5 April 1786. In the 18th century Alphonsus Liguori built his book The Glories of Mary on its outline. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a> added it to the prayers said at the end of Low Mass; it is traditionally sung at the end of a priest's funeral Mass and said at the end of the Rosary. In 1687 the German priest Johann Georg Seidenbusch, inspired by the pilgrimages to Aufhausen Priory, published a hymn whose salutations come mostly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salve-regina">Salve Regina</a>; its English version, &quot;Hail, Holy Queen enthroned above&quot;, first appeared in The Roman Missal in 1884.</p><p>Victoria, Palestrina, Josquin, Lassus, Charpentier, Pergolesi, Alessandro Scarlatti, Vivaldi, Zelenka, Handel and Liszt all set the text; Schubert wrote no fewer than seven versions, Poulenc one in 1941, and Arvo Pärt one first performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen-cathedral">Essen Cathedral</a> in 2002. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salve-regina">Salve Regina</a> University, founded by the Sisters of Mercy in 1934, is named after the hymn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The hymn greets Mary as queen and mother and turns to her for help and mercy. It is sung as an evening prayer at the close of the day, and its tone is humble and trusting. Later devotion read it as presenting Mary as a &quot;Gate of Heaven&quot; given to humankind.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Salve, Regína,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hail, Queen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Mater misericórdiæ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother of mercy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">vita, dulcédo et spes nostra, salve.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our life, sweetness and hope, hail.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ad te clamámus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To you we cry out,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">éxsules filii Evæ,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>exiled children of Eve,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ad te suspirámus geméntes et flentes</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To you we sigh, groaning and weeping</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">in hac lacrimárum valle.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in this valley of tears.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Eia ergo, advocáta nostra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come then, our advocate,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">illos tuos misericórdes óculos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>those merciful eyes of yours</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ad nos convérte.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>turn towards us.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Et Iesum, benedíctum fructum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And Jesus, the blessed fruit</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ventris tui,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of your womb,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">nobis, post hoc exsílium osténde.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>show to us after this exile.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">O clemens, o pia, o dulcis Virgo María.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O merciful, O kind, O sweet Virgin Mary.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional (Latin text as in the Vatican's edition). Text from <a href="https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Salve_Regina" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>lines 1 and 3: 'Salve' is a greeting ('hail', literally 'be well'); it opens line 1 and closes line 3.</li><li>line 8: 'Eia' is an exclamation of urging, 'come now', 'ah then'; 'advocata' is one who pleads another's case.</li><li>line 14: 'clemens' is merciful, mild; 'pia' is dutiful, loving, kind. This closing line is often said to be a later addition, sometimes credited to Bernard of Clairvaux (disputed).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="4PQy2RlqdmU" data-title="Salve Regina: Gregorian chant, solemn tone"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Gregorian chant, solemn tone</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Gregorian Chant Academy. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4PQy2RlqdmU" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gegrüßet seist du, Königin (Johann Georg Seidenbusch)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1687</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as (a hymn of salutations taken mostly from the Salve Regina)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Salve Regina coelitum</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">after 1687</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation (Latin version of Seidenbusch's hymn)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hail, Holy Queen enthroned above</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1884</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation (English version of Seidenbusch's hymn, The Roman Missal)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Glories of Mary (Alphonsus Liguori)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">18th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as (the book's outline)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Settings by Victoria, Palestrina, Josquin, Lassus, Charpentier (5), Clérambault, Pergolesi (3), Porpora (3), Alessandro Scarlatti, Vivaldi, Zelenka, Hasse (3), Handel and Liszt</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">15th-19th centuries</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Seven settings (Franz Schubert)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">19th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Salve Regina (Francis Poulenc)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1941</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Salve Regina (Arvo Pärt)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2002</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Salve Regina, organ work in seven movements (Olivier Latry)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2007</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (reflects the hymn's lines in Gregorian chant)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Oh What a Circus (Evita, Andrew Lloyd Webber and Tim Rice)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1976</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (the words of the first verse as a repeating theme)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sister Act (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1992</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as (Hail, Holy Queen enthroned above, sung by a nuns' choir, then in a soul and gospel arrangement)</span></li></ul></section>
